Since I launched the site, I've heard variations of the same question: Where can we read your fiction?

I'm sharing excerpts here of stories set in Lesotho, which remain dear to me even as my writing has evolved to include other settings. I can't share the full stories, but I thought it would be fun to share these snippets of a long, joyful writing life. I hope to point you to the full versions published somewhere, in time.

\
My commitment in my early writing life was to write about everyday realities, without either the burden of truth or much thought to popular plots or themes. As one of my writing mentors put it, I simply sought to do justice to capturing slices of life in Lesotho through fiction.

\
\
Alongside placing these stories, I'm also working toward publication of two novels and a nonfiction book about grief. However long the road, I stay believing you will meet these stories.

---

**From** ***The River Knows***

Many longed to leave your small village, which cowered like a squatter on the edge of a hill. Likhakeng looked misplaced, as though the homesteads might at any minute tip over into debris of '*malikotoana* pots, enamel crockery, and sacks of *khoahla* maize. Young women desired to flee from marriages that could, as you walked home from fetching water or firewood, come tumbling on you like a Nigerian synagogue one September afternoon. The young men left behind pitch-black rural nights to endure long days in the equally dark underbelly of the city of lights, building bounty they'd only ever taste in crumbs. Some young women dreamed of servitude in the city's suburbs. But none desired departure more than you. Away from the Seaka river, to no particular place. Just away.

\
The day your aunt's Jeep lurched into the yard, you and your maroon suitcase stood on the stoep outside your parents' two-roomed *huis*. Rakhali had been one of those who left. You liked that she did not leave to be chained to a sewing machine in a Chinese factory in Maseru. She hadn't escaped from working in the family fields only to go and work in the *Boers'* fields in the background of Cape Town, as your mother mocked other relatives.

\
"How are you, Tšeki?" She inched the car so close you retreated a little, convinced she might drive right through the greying door.

---

**From** ***Not Compatible with Decency***

Mosola, his *Lefitori* blanket thrown over his shoulder in the new way, starts off with some of the only things about Khahliso that Reitumetse can confirm to be true. Book smart. Leader in his school days and in his work as a structural engineer. Car fanatic. Reitumetse zones out, until her husband's friend moves closer to the microphone, tone descending to boyishness: "Allow me to speak truthfully about this man. About my friend." Reitumetse feels two things at once — hunger for Khahliso to be exposed and the urge to dump the porridge they near-force-fed her that morning into the handbag she's still clutching.

"Khahliso was always charming, am I right?" Booming, careless laughter rises from the floor.

Mosola smiles with self-satisfaction. "When we were growing up, he got so much attention that we relied on his excess for our own supply." He is certainly going to say it; Reitumetse is sure. "He could never meet the demand, so he'd pass on the surplus to some of us who had nothing." No exposé. More laughter.

Beside Reitumetse, 'Ma-Anna leans across to the other aunt-in-law, 'Meisi. Her downturned lips and flaring nostrils suggest she does not share the audience's amusement.

"What is this one saying?"

"*Nxa*," is the only tongue-clicking response 'Meisi offers.

---

**From** ***Predisposed***

’Mangoane almost dropped the enamel bowl of gravediggers' meat nestled atop her head when she saw me. I called her ’Mangoane, mother’s sister, but the precise trajectory of our relation I could not readily articulate. She was most notorious for biting off another relative's lower lip and refusing to give it back to be sewn on, so that years after their fight, the other woman descended to her grave missing her lower lip. Guarding me with her eyes as though I might mysteriously vanish, she lowered the bowl to the brown earth, her lips stretched in a gap-toothed smile. \

\
\
She placed a stern, mouth half-open kiss on my lips. "So good to see you, child of my brother. We will talk later, but see what you can do. There are pumpkins in the tent." \

\
\
Mangaone’s bottom upped and downed as she made her way to the big *moluoane* tree under which the men gathered. It did not surprise me that it was empty, while the others were abuzz with women buried in less torturous tasks: sorting the samp, washing pots, slicing the cabbage. Anything but the pile of pumpkin that lay untouched in the middle of the tent. I retrieved the newly purchased kitchen knife from my sling bag.

