**From** ***The Bang***\*

She wore her yellow dress the day they came, a happy-eyed sunflower, carrot braids tied back with a brown headband. His uncle had called the night before to say they would come to the house to respond to the letter informing them of the "damage" to her by their son. Mosa just wanted the traditional formalities out of their way as she smoothed her thick eyebrows in front of her bedroom mirror. Their talks would give way for her to follow the natural path.

\
The man whose child she was now carrying, unlike many, never expressed concern about her having had five-year-old Thuto before she met him. She and Moliko had been together for two years, and with this happy accident, his family's response would be nothing but to propose a wedding between the two. She was ready, ready especially for the moment after their discussion, when she would be called in to meet her future in-laws.

\**Previously published*

---

**From** ***Those Who Are Under Have Spoken***

"Speak to your wife Ntate," Rangoane Mohlolo egged, swinging the blanket to free his hands and gesture toward 'Matšotleho as though if he did not point at her, Moleko would not know which one of them was his wife. "Remember, you called us here to support you, not to speak for you."

\
"Yes, Rangoane," Moleko purred.

\
"Then speak, child of a man. Speak, son," Rangoane Monyane boomed, mouth freed from the blanket's cover and voice more voluminous than build, as always.

\
"Those who are under have spoken, my wife."

\
Moleko's intonation and how articulately he laid the Sesotho on his tongue shocked 'Matšotleho more than his mention of ancestors.

\
She did not intend to sound sarcastic, but she did. "What have those who are under said?"

---

**From** ***My Family's Keeper***

When they tell you that, as a married woman, you must bow to your husband, they don't let you pick and choose when and for what you will bow. You bow. You don't even just bow to him. The day I got married, in the evening during *litlhobohano*, my own mother turned on me before the approving eyes of my husband's family. "If your new mother flies with a broom in the night, you join her. We don't want to see you running back home saying these people do this and that. Our home is no longer your home. You are now one of these people, and as they do, you will do."

Now, I feel like doing anything but bowing to this stranger as I wobble away, depleted.

---

